(4198402, 599212, 70828, '<td valign="top">Of COURSE I let the spider live. He and his buddies help keep down the mosquito and fly population.</td>', 'Of COURSE I let the spider live. He and his buddies help keep down the mosquito and fly population.', '2007-10-26 21:21:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198426, 600009, 81530, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club<br/> <br/>The first thought that came to mind when I saw this photograph was that it is very technically sound. The bright side-lighting does an excellent job of bringing out all of the details in the mushroom. The sharpness of the edge of the fungus, as well as the bug, are both excellent.<br/> <br/>The rule of thirds has been used very well as the bug is at the intersection of these imaginary lines. I also like how the background is completely out of focus because it let''s us focus on the main subject.<br/> <br/>The one thing that takes away from this photo, is the mushroom stem. Because the stem is long and vertical, our eye is drawn away from the bug and the mushroom top. If this were cropped tighter to focus on the items at the top, or cropped to be a horizontal photo with only half of the stem, I think this image would be a lot stronger.<br/> <br/>Good shot overall. Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions, feel free to PM me.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club The first thought that came to mind when I saw this photograph was that it is very technically sound. The bright side-lighting does an excellent job of bringing out all of the details in the mushroom. The sharpness of the edge of the fungus, as well as the bug, are both excellent. The rule of thirds has been used very well as the bug is at the intersection of these imaginary lines. I also like how the background is completely out of focus because it let''s us focus on the main subject. The one thing that takes away from this photo, is the mushroom stem. Because the stem is long and vertical, our eye is drawn away from the bug and the mushroom top. If this were cropped tighter to focus on the items at the top, or cropped to be a horizontal photo with only half of the stem, I think this image would be a lot stronger. Good shot overall. Keep up the good work. If you have any questions, feel free to PM me.', '2007-10-26 21:38:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198449, 599946, 81530, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>My first impression when I saw this photograph is that I LOVE the web. It feels like we are right on top of it looking at the spider. The contrast of the bright webbing and the dark background is amazing. The depth of field is very good. The bokeh really helps the depth of field. Great job using a low f/stop.<br/> <br/>The composition is very good as well. There is plenty of negative space around the spider, and the rule of thirds works well, as the spiders "hump" falls right where the imaginary lines would be.<br/> <br/>I realize how difficult it must have been to get this shot, but I think the focus could be sharper. In a technical challenge, such as macro, viewers votes are directly related to sharpness. Had the spider been sharper, I think this photo would have done exceptionally well.<br/> <br/>As a wish list, or possible next steps, it would be nice to see the spiders face. In this photograph, his facade is blocked by his legs.<br/> <br/>All around, a very nice photo. Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions, please feel free to PM me.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club My first impression when I saw this photograph is that I LOVE the web. It feels like we are right on top of it looking at the spider. The contrast of the bright webbing and the dark background is amazing. The depth of field is very good. The bokeh really helps the depth of field. Great job using a low f/stop. The composition is very good as well. There is plenty of negative space around the spider, and the rule of thirds works well, as the spiders "hump" falls right where the imaginary lines would be. I realize how difficult it must have been to get this shot, but I think the focus could be sharper. In a technical challenge, such as macro, viewers votes are directly related to sharpness. Had the spider been sharper, I think this photo would have done exceptionally well. As a wish list, or possible next steps, it would be nice to see the spiders face. In this photograph, his facade is blocked by his legs. All around, a very nice photo. Keep up the good work. If you have any questions, please feel free to PM me.', '2007-10-26 21:52:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198480, 599510, 81530, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>My first impression when I saw this photograph is that it is very technically sound. The lighting is very nice as there are bright highlights and dark blacks. The focus is very strong as everything except the butterfly''s antennae is in focus. The butterfly''s scales and eyes are very sharp. Well done.<br/> <br/>Because you used a high ISO (1600), the grain is noticeable. It doesn''t bother me that much in this photo, but it is something you might want to keep in mind for the future. A photograph at 1/125th at f/11 and an ISO of 400 would be very sharp and have a lot less grain.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, you have used the rule of thirds very well, as the butterfly''s head is exactly where the imaginary lines would meet. You have also cropped carefully around the flower, as there is plenty of negative space. I don''t mind cropping through the butterfly because it "looks deliberate". The photograph is a little right heavy because of the direction the flower is pointing, as well as the butterfly''s wings, but I would still give it a high score.<br/> <br/>Great shot overall. Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions, please feel free to PM me.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club My first impression when I saw this photograph is that it is very technically sound. The lighting is very nice as there are bright highlights and dark blacks. The focus is very strong as everything except the butterfly''s antennae is in focus. The butterfly''s scales and eyes are very sharp. Well done. Because you used a high ISO (1600), the grain is noticeable. It doesn''t bother me that much in this photo, but it is something you might want to keep in mind for the future. A photograph at 1/125th at f/11 and an ISO of 400 would be very sharp and have a lot less grain. Compositionally, you have used the rule of thirds very well, as the butterfly''s head is exactly where the imaginary lines would meet. You have also cropped carefully around the flower, as there is plenty of negative space. I don''t mind cropping through the butterfly because it "looks deliberate". The photograph is a little right heavy because of the direction the flower is pointing, as well as the butterfly''s wings, but I would still give it a high score. Great shot overall. Keep up the good work. If you have any questions, please feel free to PM me.', '2007-10-26 22:14:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198511, 597321, 81530, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>My first thought when I saw this photograph was that the colors are very nice. The deep reds and yellows of the flower are very colorful. The lighting is good, as there are definitive highlights and shadows on the anther.<br/> <br/>From a technical stand point, I think you could benefit from using a slower shutter speed. This would allow a larger f/stop, and therefore you could get more of the pollen grains in focus.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, I like that you have divided the photo up into thirds horizontally. Overall, however, I find that the crop is very tight. Since the pollen grains are the main focus, I think they could use a little more negative space at the top and bottom of the page. Alternatively, you could crop even closer to focus on just the pollen grains, in which case, you could crop out the top part of the anthers. Either way, it is always important to make your crops look deliberate.<br/> <br/>Overall, nice idea for detail. Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions, feel free to PM me.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club My first thought when I saw this photograph was that the colors are very nice. The deep reds and yellows of the flower are very colorful. The lighting is good, as there are definitive highlights and shadows on the anther. From a technical stand point, I think you could benefit from using a slower shutter speed. This would allow a larger f/stop, and therefore you could get more of the pollen grains in focus. Compositionally, I like that you have divided the photo up into thirds horizontally. Overall, however, I find that the crop is very tight. Since the pollen grains are the main focus, I think they could use a little more negative space at the top and bottom of the page. Alternatively, you could crop even closer to focus on just the pollen grains, in which case, you could crop out the top part of the anthers. Either way, it is always important to make your crops look deliberate. Overall, nice idea for detail. Keep up the good work. If you have any questions, feel free to PM me.', '2007-10-26 22:30:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198723, 600373, 40718, '<td valign="top">Hey, I haven''t had a chance to say congrats on the first ribbon.  I bet there will be more... :)</td>', 'Hey, I haven''t had a chance to say congrats on the first ribbon.  I bet there will be more... :)', '2007-10-27 00:37:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198731, 599408, 60862, '<td valign="top">I like the lighting, color, and DOF in this shot.  The composition bothers me.  This is the kind of subject where centered works well for me, but I''d rather it was truly centered instead of just a little off to the right.  Congrats on your new 5th-best score!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-10-26 23:40:27.</i></td>', 'I like the lighting, color, and DOF in this shot.  The composition bothers me.  This is the kind of subject where centered works well for me, but I''d rather it was truly centered instead of just a little off to the right.  Congrats on your new 5th-best score!', '2007-10-27 00:40:06.000000', '2007-10-27 00:40:27.000000', 0),
(4198740, 600337, 60862, '<td valign="top">I voted this is a 6, Julianne.  I like the composition and choice of subject, and the mushroom''s color is reflected nicely in the needles.  I tend to prefer higher contrast, though.  Also, as a macro, I look for really crisp details.  I see them in the lichen, but not in the mushroom.  Of course, DOF is a major challenge in macros.  The lichen actually looks a little oversharpened, although that might be an artifact of the small image size.  I wonder how this would look if you did a gaussian blur on a duplicate layer and then set the opacity to about 50%?  It might add a nice glow to the mushroom.</td>', 'I voted this is a 6, Julianne.  I like the composition and choice of subject, and the mushroom''s color is reflected nicely in the needles.  I tend to prefer higher contrast, though.  Also, as a macro, I look for really crisp details.  I see them in the lichen, but not in the mushroom.  Of course, DOF is a major challenge in macros.  The lichen actually looks a little oversharpened, although that might be an artifact of the small image size.  I wonder how this would look if you did a gaussian blur on a duplicate layer and then set the opacity to about 50%?  It might add a nice glow to the mushroom.', '2007-10-27 00:44:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198742, 599586, 60862, '<td valign="top">I voted this a 6.  I like the diagonal composition and the details in the stem and bottom of the crown.  As with many photos, I think I would''ve liked it even better with more contrast.  I think you did quite well lacking a real macro lens.</td>', 'I voted this a 6.  I like the diagonal composition and the details in the stem and bottom of the crown.  As with many photos, I think I would''ve liked it even better with more contrast.  I think you did quite well lacking a real macro lens.', '2007-10-27 00:46:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198748, 600344, 60862, '<td valign="top">Interesting shot, Don.  The leaf''s veins do a nice job of framing the galls and driving my eye down to them.  The strong color is very pretty, too.  Looks like you got some nice detail on the leaf on the right side.  My main problem is that I like macros to have a fair amount of sharp detail to explore.  As you know, I highly appreciate deliberate blur, but this feels more like a lack of DOF control in a macro, which is definitely one of the hard things to get right.  I look forward to more explorations of your sexy lens. ;)</td>', 'Interesting shot, Don.  The leaf''s veins do a nice job of framing the galls and driving my eye down to them.  The strong color is very pretty, too.  Looks like you got some nice detail on the leaf on the right side.  My main problem is that I like macros to have a fair amount of sharp detail to explore.  As you know, I highly appreciate deliberate blur, but this feels more like a lack of DOF control in a macro, which is definitely one of the hard things to get right.  I look forward to more explorations of your sexy lens. ;)', '2007-10-27 00:49:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198757, 600363, 60862, '<td valign="top">Interesting composition, Rob.  I like the intersecting corners and placement of the dots (pips?).  I think your score got hit for two things:<br/>1) There''s no real detail in the surface of the die.  That''s just a fact of life with this subject.  But one of the joys of macros, at least for me, is seeing details I wouldn''t normally be able to see.<br/> <br/>2) The lack of detail makes the whole face look blown out, which is a sizable portion of the photo to have no detail.<br/> <br/>Still an interesting shot, though!</td>', 'Interesting composition, Rob.  I like the intersecting corners and placement of the dots (pips?).  I think your score got hit for two things:1) There''s no real detail in the surface of the die.  That''s just a fact of life with this subject.  But one of the joys of macros, at least for me, is seeing details I wouldn''t normally be able to see. 2) The lack of detail makes the whole face look blown out, which is a sizable portion of the photo to have no detail. Still an interesting shot, though!', '2007-10-27 00:52:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198763, 599212, 60862, '<td valign="top">I like the composition and what detail is there.  But for a macro, it''s not sharp enough to give me things to explore.  Maybe bumping up the contrast would''ve helped your score, but if you like it, then it''s good enough as is. :)</td>', 'I like the composition and what detail is there.  But for a macro, it''s not sharp enough to give me things to explore.  Maybe bumping up the contrast would''ve helped your score, but if you like it, then it''s good enough as is. :)', '2007-10-27 00:54:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198770, 599666, 60862, '<td valign="top">I liked this composition.  There''s plenty of detail and interesting areas to explore.</td>', 'I liked this composition.  There''s plenty of detail and interesting areas to explore.', '2007-10-27 00:55:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198782, 599673, 60862, '<td valign="top">Pretty cool to hear where this was.  Nice composition of an interesting subject.  The lighting''s quite good, and I love the title.  Amazing that you avoided any score under 4, to boot!</td>', 'Pretty cool to hear where this was.  Nice composition of an interesting subject.  The lighting''s quite good, and I love the title.  Amazing that you avoided any score under 4, to boot!', '2007-10-27 01:01:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198786, 600313, 60862, '<td valign="top">As always, Shez, your eye for composition is terrific.  I like the colors, too.  In a macro challenge, though, I think people are looking for razor-sharp details, and this is quite soft.  It''s lovely, just not going to do well with DPC voters in this type of challenge.</td>', 'As always, Shez, your eye for composition is terrific.  I like the colors, too.  In a macro challenge, though, I think people are looking for razor-sharp details, and this is quite soft.  It''s lovely, just not going to do well with DPC voters in this type of challenge.', '2007-10-27 01:03:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198792, 599611, 60862, '<td valign="top">Lea, I like the composition and the color.  They both remind me of more traditional photos and art.  My big problem is the blotchiness of the background.  I don''t know whether that''s noise, JPG compression artifacts, or something else, but it distracts me quite a bit.  I''d also suggest trying it with the flame centered.  I normally like off-center, but here it just feels like empty space to me.</td>', 'Lea, I like the composition and the color.  They both remind me of more traditional photos and art.  My big problem is the blotchiness of the background.  I don''t know whether that''s noise, JPG compression artifacts, or something else, but it distracts me quite a bit.  I''d also suggest trying it with the flame centered.  I normally like off-center, but here it just feels like empty space to me.', '2007-10-27 01:05:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198799, 600302, 60862, '<td valign="top">You got an 8 from me!  I agree with you about the lighting - it''s terrific!  I also really like the composition, with the leaves drawing my eye to the lower left.</td>', 'You got an 8 from me!  I agree with you about the lighting - it''s terrific!  I also really like the composition, with the leaves drawing my eye to the lower left.', '2007-10-27 01:07:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198803, 599620, 60862, '<td valign="top">A nice experiment, Julie.  The little shapes are interesting.  I think the wide aspect ratio hurt your score, though - it doesn''t have as much impact when it fills so little of the screen. I love how your commenters did guess at what it was - isn''t it fun when they engage like that? :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-10-27 00:10:09.</i></td>', 'A nice experiment, Julie.  The little shapes are interesting.  I think the wide aspect ratio hurt your score, though - it doesn''t have as much impact when it fills so little of the screen. I love how your commenters did guess at what it was - isn''t it fun when they engage like that? :)', '2007-10-27 01:09:30.000000', '2007-10-27 01:10:09.000000', 0),
(4198809, 600320, 60862, '<td valign="top">I''m glad you like it, Brian.  Very cool story.  I think the score reflects the fuzziness and low contrast, plus the yellow cast.  Also, I think photos of other people''s art don''t generally get people excited.<br/> <br/>You might try a different composition and lighting scheme for the current "Something Old" challenge.</td>', 'I''m glad you like it, Brian.  Very cool story.  I think the score reflects the fuzziness and low contrast, plus the yellow cast.  Also, I think photos of other people''s art don''t generally get people excited. You might try a different composition and lighting scheme for the current "Something Old" challenge.', '2007-10-27 01:12:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(4198867, 597542, 82707, '<td valign="top">Wow!  Great image and amazing colours!  Congrats on a ribbon!</td>', 'Wow!  Great image and amazing colours!  Congrats on a ribbon!', '2007-10-27 01:42:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4199614, 600344, 61651, '<td valign="top">The leaf texture looks like crocodile skin - and yes, those are some quite hairy galls.  Hmmm... now I''m getting all sorts of ideas about the Garden of Eden - your lens corrupts again.  Get thee behind me, Satan.</td>', 'The leaf texture looks like crocodile skin - and yes, those are some quite hairy galls.  Hmmm... now I''m getting all sorts of ideas about the Garden of Eden - your lens corrupts again.  Get thee behind me, Satan.', '2007-10-27 15:22:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(4199615, 599586, 61651, '<td valign="top">I thought butter churner too - I am completely surprised.  Amazing textures and good placement of shadows - I agree you did very well with lighting here.</td>', 'I thought butter churner too - I am completely surprised.  Amazing textures and good placement of shadows - I agree you did very well with lighting here.', '2007-10-27 15:25:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(4199973, 597542, 5112, '<td valign="top">Jason, congratulations!</td>', 'Jason, congratulations!', '2007-10-27 20:01:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(4200414, 600119, 45854, '<td valign="top">Perfect!! Congratulations!</td>', 'Perfect!! Congratulations!', '2007-10-28 00:17:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(4200420, 599340, 67841, '<td valign="top">Excellent shot!</td>', 'Excellent shot!', '2007-10-28 00:19:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4200943, 599983, 79720, '<td valign="top">Wow, I''m sorry you ended with 0 comments during voting.<br/> <br/>I might have pulled back from this subject a bit, and lined it up on the rule of thirds. The image looks a touch noisy, probably due to a long zoom and the 200 ISO. Run it through Neat Image, Noise Ninja or similar noise reduction software. The lighting was a challenge for you, since it looks like it may have been overcast...if you played with the levels, you might have been able to bring some contrast in to the composition.</td>', 'Wow, I''m sorry you ended with 0 comments during voting. I might have pulled back from this subject a bit, and lined it up on the rule of thirds. The image looks a touch noisy, probably due to a long zoom and the 200 ISO. Run it through Neat Image, Noise Ninja or similar noise reduction software. The lighting was a challenge for you, since it looks like it may have been overcast...if you played with the levels, you might have been able to bring some contrast in to the composition.', '2007-10-28 04:35:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(4201045, 598869, 48228, '<td valign="top">Beautiful and actually quite stunning.....</td>', 'Beautiful and actually quite stunning.....', '2007-10-28 08:21:26.000000', NULL, 0),
